<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Finno-Soviet Treaty of 1948</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1948">1948</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Agreement of Friendship, Cooperation and Mutual Assistance, YYA Treaty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Finno-Soviet Treaty of 1948, formally the Agreement of Friendship, Cooperation and Mutual Assistance, was signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on 6 April 1948. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> undertook to resist any attack by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> or its allies on itself, or on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> through Finnish territory, while the treaty acknowledged Finland's wish to stay outside great-power conflicts. Renewed several times, it framed Finnish foreign policy until it lapsed in 1992.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/finno-soviet-treaty-of-1948.jpg" alt="Finno-Soviet Treaty of 1948" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Government of Finland · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Finno-Soviet_Treaty_of_1948.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
